{
  "audit": "Transformer Zero-Sequence, Grounding, and YNyn0 Non-Overgeneralization Audit",
  "key_finding": "A YNyn0 transformer does NOT intrinsically block zero-sequence current. In OpenDSS with a 3-wire positive-sequence Generator (no neutral return on DER_LV), I0 on the 416 V side is negligible (~0.005 A) only because the inverter side is open to zero sequence. When a 4-wire/grounded neutral path exists on the inverter side, YNyn0 transmits zero-sequence current directly through the transformer (e.g., Bus 822 noon: I0_416V rises from 0.005 A to 0.306 A with I0_inv = 0.667 A, and phase powers skew by 0.121 kW). Conversely, Dyn always traps zero sequence in its delta winding (0.572 A at Bus 822 noon), while Ynd structurally blocks I0 on the 416 V side (0.000 A) even with a 4-wire inverter, at the cost of leaving the 416 V side without a neutral/ground reference.",
